 ## 中文摘要
 
-多智能体工作流通过角色分工、依赖和验收门槛组织交付，但这些规则为何仍不能让团队自主确定下一步？本文以Agent Center中的工作项推进片段为分析单位，比较行动要求、责任解释、人工补充及后续状态，并以结构统计和合成动作实验检验解释边界。案例显示，协调失效集中在职责到具体行动的转换：分配前提没有落实为协调者自己的动作，相同合并职责指向不同分支，局部验收完成也不能代表剩余交付义务消失。两个完整案例区间的28条消息中，12条来自用户，其中5条直接补充或质询责任、对象及操作顺序。独立验收、下游事实核查和合格NO-GO报告则提供了有用的质量边界。256次合成响应中，两道题存在状态矛盾或合同缺失；排除其全部条件后，事后字段诊断中两模型均96/96正确，严格格式得分分别为91/96和96/96。全部输入已明示自主准备与门槛解释规则，因此这些结果只说明协议给定后的短题表现，不能独立验证长期流程机制。部署中可见的缺口仍是行动关系需要人工补足。本文主张以可执行工作项和剩余义务交接组织流程，保留增加证据的检查，并以无提醒推进和交付质量评价简化效果。研究为单部署探索，尚未人工复核。
+多智能体工作流已有角色、依赖和验收门槛，为何仍会在交接处停下？本文结合Agent Center案例比较、结构统计、动作探针与24次真实多轮执行，区分行动关系缺失、有效质量检查和重复协调。历史案例显示，人工需要补足动作主体、对象、阶段及合同；两个完整区间28条消息中有12条用户消息，其中5条直接纠正或质询这些关系。新增对照保持相同四类工件，比较两／四worker职责及分表／联表表示。代码实现24/24通过独立终验，完整交付23/24。四角色在全部12对中增加总决策，Sonnet与Haiku平均分别多6.67与8.50次，未增加实现通过数。唯一未闭合案例中，控制者因未直接见到审查者回执而否定其存在，审查者则拒绝引用未收到原始记录的控制者回执；重复审查用尽启动预算，发布步骤未被执行。机制解释由原生回执与版本核对支持，但尚未通过共享凭据干预分离因果。结论是工作流需要同时绑定动作和证据认可关系；增加角色及复核次数本身不能保证更可靠交付。相同任务的其它条件完成，不支持四角色必然停摆或联表具有稳定收益。明显仪器缺陷影响的旧轮全量排除并保留；研究仍属小样本探索，尚未人工复核。
 
 **关键词：**多 Agent 协作；责任闭合；工作流；独立验收；agent harness
 
 ## Abstract
 
-Multi-agent workflows organize delivery through roles, dependencies, and acceptance gates, yet these rules may leave the next action unresolved. This study asks why a team with defined responsibilities still requires human direction and which coordination boundaries remain useful. We analyze work-item transitions in Agent Center, comparing required actions, responsibility interpretations, human contributions, and subsequent records. Descriptive workflow measures and synthetic action-selection experiments examine the scope of the proposed explanation. The cases locate coordination failures at the transition from general duties to situated action: assignment requirements were not treated as the coordinator's own next step, shared merge duties referred to different branches, and local acceptance did not settle remaining delivery obligations. Two complete case windows contained 28 messages, including 12 human messages; five supplied or questioned responsibility, objects, or operation order. Independent testing, downstream fact checking, and valid NO-GO reports provided useful quality boundaries. Of 256 synthetic responses, two scenarios had contradictory states or missing contracts. After excluding all their conditions, a post-hoc field diagnostic found correct semantic choices in 96/96 responses per model, while strict-format scores were 91/96 and 96/96. All inputs explicitly prescribed autonomous preparation and gate interpretation. These results therefore concern compliance with a supplied protocol, not a test of long-running workflow mechanisms. The deployment cases still show action relations completed through human intervention. Workflow design should make actionable ownership and remaining obligations explicit, retain evidence-producing checks, and evaluate simplification by unprompted progress and delivery quality. This single-deployment study has not been human-verified.
+Why do multi-agent workflows stall at handoffs despite having roles, dependencies, and acceptance gates? This study combines Agent Center case comparisons, descriptive measures, action probes, and 24 actual multi-turn executions to distinguish incomplete action relations, useful verification, and repeated coordination. In historical cases, humans supplied missing actors, objects, stages, and contracts; two complete windows contained 28 messages, including 12 human messages, five of which corrected or questioned these relations. New controlled executions kept four required artifacts fixed while comparing two versus four worker functions and split versus joined relation views. All 24 implementations passed independent acceptance, while 23 achieved complete delivery. Four-role workflows required more total model decisions in all 12 matched pairs, averaging 6.67 additional decisions for Sonnet and 8.50 for Haiku, without increasing implementation passes. In the sole incomplete episode, the controller rejected genuine reviewer receipts absent from its own tool history, while reviewers declined to cite controller receipts whose original records they had not received. Repeated review consumed the worker-start budget before release integration. Receipt and version checks support this process explanation, but no shared-receipt intervention has isolated its causal contribution. Workflows therefore need explicit relations for both action and evidence acceptance; additional roles and reviews alone do not ensure reliable delivery. Other conditions on the same task completed, opposing claims of inevitable four-role failure or stable gains from a joined view. Instrument-affected prior outcomes were retained and excluded. This small-sample exploratory study has not been human-verified.
 
 **Keywords:** multi-agent collaboration; accountability; workflow; independent review; agent harness
 
